russian president vladimir putin wanted  to contain nato but since the war broke  out in ukraine we've been hearing a lot  of this he wants less nato he's getting  more nato he wanted to divide those is  getting more united alliance and that is  certainly true just a few years ago the  north atlantic treaty organization which  was created after world war ii to serve  as a check on soviet expansion was  struggling to portray a united front  tonight there is worldwide fallout after  president trump once again raised doubts  about his commitment to nato macron said  nato was experiencing quote brain death  now putin's invasion of ukraine hasn't  just strengthened the bond of nato  countries or their commitment to article  5 which stipulates that an attack on one  is an attack on all putin's war is  actually making the alliance stronger in  tangible ways particularly on its  eastern flank in the country's closest  to russia  here are three ways that nato has  responded to the war in ukraine first  the alliance is significantly upgrading  its arsenal of weapons one way this is  happening is through increased defense  spending germany is the most notable  example pledging to almost double what  it was spending before the war it has  already announced that it wants to buy  state-of-the-art drones from israel and  f-35 warplanes from the u.s to deter  russian nuclear threats poland and  estonia have also pledged to spend more  while other members are currently  hashing out new spending proposals  another way nato members are upgrading  their arsenals is through their huge  operation to resupply ukrainian forces  as nato countries funnel weapons and  supplies to ukraine many are seeking the  latest and greatest us-made weapons to  refill their depleted stockpiles one  example of this so-called upgrading is  slovakia the country already received  american-made patriot air defense  systems from germany and the netherlands  so that it could send its soviet-era  s-300 air defense systems to ukraine  secondly nato has responded to the war  with more boots on the ground nato  didn't deploy combat troops to the  eastern part of the alliance until  russia annexed crimea in 2014 but in  response to what nato calls russian  aggression it created four multinational  battle groups in estonia latvia  lithuania and poland now due to russia's  invasion of ukraine the alliance has  agreed to add four more adding battle  groups in bulgaria hungary romania and  slovakia but here's the thing nato says  these troops are rotating and therefore  not considered permanent  now that could change  so far nato hasn't permanently based  troops on the eastern flank because a  quarter century ago nato and russia  struck an agreement per the nato-russia  founding act nato said it wouldn't put  troops in its new eastern members and  nato and russia committed not to  threaten each other use force against  each other or use force against any  other state its sovereignty territorial  integrity or political independence now  that russia has invaded ukraine western  officials say that agreement is  nullified and that the alliance can put  troops wherever it sees fit and last but  not least as a result of this war nato  could expand even further  in the officially neutral countries of  finland and sweden polls show that for  the very first time a majority of the  populations support joining the alliance  we have a change situation in  both in  finland and sweden this is something we  are now  open to a discussion with our parliament  finland shares the european union's  longest border with russia so it has  tried to avoid antagonizing moscow but  russia's invasion of ukraine has alarmed  the population russia has explicitly  warned finland and sweden not to join  nato nevertheless the two countries both  participated in an emergency nato  meeting in march  so nato is clearly getting stronger but  one country that's unlikely to join  anytime soon ukraine countries that want  to join must meet strict criteria and  ukraine is far from meeting those  requirements in recent weeks ukrainian  president vladimir zelinsky has  acknowledged that ukraine is unlikely to  become a nato member anytime soon if you  thought this piece was informative hit  the like button and subscribe to the  channel for more stories like this i'll  see you soon
 

